e logic. The fundamental behavior is that chgrp follows symlinks by default unless `-h` is specified, and the recursive modes have their own rules for how they handle symlinks during traversal.\n\nThe current implementation uses `Lchown` everywhere, which works for the existing tests because they only test regular files where `Lchown` and `Chown` behave identically. I need to switch the default to `Chown` and only use `Lchown` when `-h` is explicitly set, while being careful not to break the existing tests. Defining the options struct...\n\nNow I need to figure out which chown function to use based on the options and whether we're processing a command-line argument, since the behavior changes depending on the symlink mode and recursion settings. The logic gets tricky because -H only follows symlinks at the top level, while -L follows everything and -P follows nothing. Examining the GNU coreutils source code to understand the actual implementation—it looks like the key difference is whether `chown` or `lchown` gets called based on the traversal mode, with `FTS_PHYSICAL` triggering `lchown` and other modes using `chown`. So the key insight is that `ch_option` determines which ownership function gets called—when it's set to `FTS_PHYSICAL`, `lchown` is used to modify the symlink itself rather than its target. With `-H`, even though `FTS_COMFOLLOW` is added to follow command-line symlinks during traversal, `ch_option` still equals `FTS_PHYSICAL`, so `lchown` gets called on those symlinks too.\n\nNow I'm seeing the full behavior pattern: `-P` uses `lchown` and never follows symlinks, `-H` uses `lchown` but follows command-line symlinks into directories, and `-L` uses `chown` and follows all symlinks. For non-recursive mode, the default is `chown` while `-h` switches to `lchown`.\n\nI'm also noticing that verbose output formatting is already partially done—the options are parsed but the diagnostic messages aren't being printed yet. Since the current task focuses on R2.1 through R2.3, I should stay focused on those requirements rather than getting into R3.1's verbose output implementation. I need to clarify my scope—I'm only implementing R2.1, R2.2, and R2.3, not the verbose/changes diagnostics from R3.1. For the symlink handling, `filepath.WalkDir` won't give me the control I need for -L (follow all symlinks) or -H (follow only command-line symlinks), so I'll need to build a custom recursive walk that respects these different traversal modes. I'm going to implement a custom recursive walk function instead since it'll be cleaner and more consistent across all the symlink-handling modes. I'll create a changeGroup function that either handles a single file or delegates to the recursive walker, and a chownFile helper that applies the appropriate dereferencing logic based on whether we're at the top level and what flags are set.\n\nWriting the implementation... Writing recursive directory traversal...\n\nNow I'm thinking through how the different flag combinations affect symlink dereferencing during recursion. With -H, only command-line arguments get dereferenced, while -L always follows symlinks to directories, and -P never follows them. The GNU coreutils implementation maps these to specific dereference modes that control the behavior throughout the walk.\n\nDefining the data structures for the implementation... Writing recursive traversal logic...\n\nActually, I'm realizing there's an important detail here—when recursively traversing with the `-R -P` flags, symlinks encountered during the walk should have `lchown` applied to them directly without following them, which is what the code does by passing `false` for the `isTopLevel` parameter on recursive calls.\n\nFor `-R -L`, the behavior flips: we use `chown` on symlinks and follow them if they point to directories, since `lstatOrStat` will use `stat` to resolve the target.\n\nThe `-R -H` case is trickier—command-line arguments should be followed with `stat` and `chown`, but during traversal we should use `lstat` and `lchown`, which `shouldFollow` handles by checking whether we're at the top level.\n\nWhen a command-line arg is a symlink to a directory under `-R -H`, we stat it to get the target's directory info, chown the target, then walk its contents—but for each child, `shouldFollow` returns false since we're no longer at the top level, so we use `lstat` and `lchown` instead.\n\nNow I'm realizing `os.ReadDir` on a symlink path will follow it and list the target directory's contents, which is what we want. But there's a subtlety with `-R -P`: if the user passes a symlink to a directory, we `lstat` it (seeing it's a symlink, not a directory), `lchown` it, and don't recurse—but GNU chgrp would actually recurse into the target directory in this case.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Actually, with `-R -P`, a symlink to a directory should be treated as a symlink, not followed, so we change the symlink itself and don't recurse. That matches what our code does.\n\nFor non-recursive mode, I need to switch from always using `lchown` to using `chown` by default (to follow symlinks), since `lchown` and `chown` are identical for regular files anyway. I need to handle both `--dereference` and `--no-dereference` flags, where the last one specified takes precedence. Since I'm processing arguments left-to-right, I can implement this by having `--dereference` clear the `noDereference` flag while `--no-dereference` sets it, so whichever comes last wins.\n\nFor the short flags like `-h`, `-H`, `-L`, and `-P`, I'm setting the appropriate options directly, and for `-R` I'm enabling recursive mode. Now I need to think through how these interact—particularly how `-R` works with the symlink-following flags, since GNU chgrp has specific rules about which flag takes precedence when recursing. traversal behavior\n- -h is irrelevant with -R (the traversal flags take precedence)\n\nActually, I think -h and -R are somewhat orthogonal in GNU coreutils. But in practice, `-R` implies `-P` which behaves like `-h` for the walk. The `-h` flag is really only meaningful without `-R`.\n\nI think the cleanest approach is:\n- For non-recursive: `noDereference` determines lchown vs chown\n- For recursive: `symlink` mode determines lchown vs chown and traversal\n\nAnd I don't need to worry about the interaction between -h and -R because the code handles them independently.\n\nNow let me think about the verbose/changes output. The current code doesn't implement diagnostic output for -v/-c. Since R3.1 is not part of my task, I'll leave this as-is. The -v/-c/-f flags are already parsed and stored in opts.\n\nWait, but the task says \"R2.1, R2.2, R2.3\" — let me re-read the task summary more carefully. The task requires implementing recursive traversal and symlink handling options for cmd/chgrp, specifically srd090 requirements R2.1, R2.2, and R2.3. So I'm only implementing R2.1 (recursive), R2.2 (dereference/no-dereference), R2.3 (-H/-L/-P). Not R3 (verbose/changes/exit codes). Good.\n\nActually, wait. The task also has acceptance criteria like \"chgrp staff file changes group to staff, matching gchgrp\" for R1.1 and R1.3, and \"chgrp -R\" for another criterion. Let me check what the full scope actually is.\n\nOK now I need to write the actual code. I need to be careful about the 40-line function limit. Let me also check what imports I need — I'll use `filepath.Join` for building child paths and `os.ReadDir` for listing directory contents.\n\nLooking at my current imports, I need to add `\"path/filepath\"` for the `filepath.Join` function.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the walk approach. For `-P` and `-H`, I only need to handle regular directories without following symlink targets (except at the top level for `-H`). For `-L`, I follow all symlinks. The key decision is which stat function to use for each command-line argument: `os.Chown` follows symlinks by default, `os.Lchown` doesn't, and for recursive walks I'll use `os.Lstat` to decide how to proceed based on the flags.\n\nWriting the modified main.go... I need to reconsider how GNU coreutils actually handles this with FTS flags. When `-H` is used with `-R`, it sets both `FTS_COMFOLLOW` and `FTS_PHYSICAL`, which means symlink arguments get followed as directories but all operations use `lstat` and `lchown`. So a symlink-to-directory argument would have its contents traversed, but I'm still uncertain whether the symlink itself gets modified or if the traversal happens differently than I'm thinking. I'm realizing there's an inconsistency in how the `-H` flag handles symlinks to directories—it would change the symlink itself with `lchown`, but then traverse into the target directory and change everything inside it, which seems contradictory. I need to verify this against actual GNU chgrp behavior to understand what's really happening. at it from a different angle. The FTS library with FTS_COMFOLLOW follows the symlink and then the FTSENT.fts_info would be FTS_D (directory) rather than FTS_SL (symlink). So the code would process it as a directory and recurse.\n\nBut what about the chown call? With FTS_PHYSICAL set (as part of -H), it uses lchown. But the path returned by FTS is the original symlink path. So `lchown(symlink_path, uid, gid)` would change the symlink's group, not the target's.\n\nWhen FTS_COMFOLLOW is set and the argument is a symlink to a directory, FTS follows it but the FTSENT still references the original symlink path, while the stat information comes from the target. The children would be the contents of the target directory. Since lchown operates on the symlink itself rather than its target, the symlink's group gets changed.\n\nRather than trying to reverse-engineer the exact behavior from source code, I should just test empirically against GNU chgrp to see what actually happens and match that.\n\nDefining the symlink handling modes...\n\nNow I'm structuring the main flow with separate functions for non-recursive and recursive cases. For the non-recursive path, I'll use either `os.Chown` or `os.Lchown` depending on whether we're dereferencing symlinks. For the recursive case, I need to determine whether to follow symlinks based on the mode and whether we're at the top level.\n\nExamining error handling...\n\nI see that `formatErr` already handles `*os.PathError` by formatting it as \"changing group of 'path': error\", but for stat failures in the recursive case, GNU chgrp would report \"cannot access 'path': error\" instead. The differential tests normalize error verbs though, so this discrepancy might not matter in practice. I'm wondering whether I should even worry about matching the exact error message for stat failures during recursive walks, or if I should just let the current error handling stand.\n\nFor the recursive walk itself, when `os.Stat` or `os.ReadDir` fails, I should report the error and continue processing other files, just like GNU chgrp does. Now I'm sketching out the revised approach where I stat the path first, report any stat errors but keep going, then apply the chown operation and handle directory recursion based on whether the path is actually a directory.\n\nWriting recursive chown logic... I'm noticing a discrepancy in how GNU chgrp reports errors — it says \"cannot access\" when stat fails in recursive mode, but \"changing group of\" when the chown itself fails. The existing error normalizer already handles both cases with a regex that matches either phrase, so the differential tests should work correctly without needing special handling. The normalizer handles both error message formats by converting them to a common \"ERROR_VERB\" token, so the differential test should still pass even though Go and GNU produce different output strings. Let me trace through what happens when `os.Lstat` fails on a nonexistent path: it returns a `PathError` with the operation and path, which gets formatted as \"changing group of '/nonexistent': no such file or directory\", but after normalization both that and GNU's \"cannot access\" format collapse to the same token, making them equivalent for testing purposes.\n\nThere's a potential issue though—`chownRecursive` might report an error from `statPath` and then attempt `applyChown` on the same path, resulting in duplicate error messages. I need to restructure the function so that if the stat fails, we report it and return early without trying to chown.\n\nRestructuring the function...\n\nBut `applyChown` also reports its own errors, and GNU chgrp intentionally continues processing even when chown fails on a directory. The question is whether we should still recurse into a directory if the chown operation itself fails on it.\n\nLooking at how GNU coreutils handles this: if we can stat the file and read its contents (which we can if we're calling ReadDir), then we should continue processing its children even if the chown fails. So the logic of continuing makes sense.\n\nI need to refactor `applyChown` to just handle the actual chown operation and error reporting, while `chownRecursive` tracks whether any errors occurred overall. Let me define `applyChown` to call the appropriate chown function based on the symlink option and return the error.\n\nDefining the applyChown function...\n\nNow I'm clarifying the behavior for each flag: with -P and -H, we use lchown for everything since FTS_PHYSICAL is set, while -L uses chown for everything. The flags only affect how FTS traverses the tree, not the actual chown calls themselves.\n\nWriting helper functions...\n\nNow I'm thinking about cycle detection for the `-R -L` case, since following symlinks can create infinite loops. `filepath.WalkDir` doesn't follow symlinks so it's safe, but my custom walk implementation needs protection. GNU chgrp uses FTS which detects cycles and reports them as errors, so I should track visited inode/device pairs to catch when we encounter a directory we've already seen.\n\nThe acceptance criteria don't explicitly require `-L` testing, so I could defer this complexity, but adding a simple visited set for `-L` mode seems like the right safety measure. Let me keep the implementation straightforward and only apply cycle detection when `-L` is actually used.\n\nFor the traversal approach, `filepath.WalkDir` always uses `os.Lstat` internally, which means it won't follow symlinks—perfect for `-P` but limiting for `-H` and `-L`. I think the cleanest solution is to stick with `os.ReadDir` for all cases to maintain consistency and have full control over symlink handling, even if it means deviating slightly from the design guideline.\n\nNow I'm setting up the core data structures: defining a symlink mode enum to represent the three behaviors (`-P`, `-H`, `-L`), and creating an options struct to hold all the configuration flags like verbose, recursive, and which symlink mode to use.\n\nDefining helper functions... Writing function implementations... Writing function definitions...\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering how to handle the error from `os.ReadDir`. It doesn't necessarily return a `*os.PathError`, so I should pass the error directly to `reportError` instead of trying to wrap it. Let me check what `reportError` expects and how it formats errors.\n\nLooking at the existing error formatting logic, it checks if the error is a `*os.PathError` and extracts the path and underlying error. For `os.ReadDir` failures, the error would come from the underlying `Open` call, which does produce a `*os.PathError`. The formatter would then display it as \"changing group of 'directory': permission denied\" or similar, which should match the expected output format.\n\nHowever, I'm realizing the normalizer regex only matches \"cannot access\" or \"changing group of\", but GNU's actual error message for a directory open failure would be \"cannot open directory\", which wouldn't be caught by the current pattern. I need to expand the normalizer to handle this case.\n\nFor now, I'll address that in the test code. I should also consider the edge case where `os.ReadDir` encounters permission issues on individual entries—but since `os.ReadDir` returns all entries at once and permission problems only surface when we try to stat them, I think the current approach is fine.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement the actual changes. I need to add the `recursive`, `noDereference`, and `symlink` fields to the options struct, handle the corresponding short and long flags in the parsing logic, and refactor `changeGroup` to dispatch to either single or recursive variants. I'll also need to create the new helper functions to handle each case.\n\nFor the `--dereference` flag, I realize it's already documented in the help text but not actually parsed. Since `--dereference` is the default behavior, I just need a `noDereference` boolean in the options—`--dereference` sets it to false while `-h` and `--no-dereference` set it to true.\n\nAs for `--preserve-root` and `--no-preserve-root`, the SRD marks this as a non-goal, so I shouldn't implement the actual behavior. But I still need to parse these flags to prevent them from being misinterpreted as GROUP arguments. I'm thinking I'll just parse them and ignore them for now, since implementing the recursive protection on `/` isn't required. I'm tracing through the argument parsing logic to see how `--preserve-root` would be handled — it doesn't match any special cases or long flags, so it would fall through and get treated as the GROUP argument instead of being recognized as a flag.\n\nLooking at this more carefully, I should just add a case to handle both `--preserve-root` and `--no-preserve-root` as no-ops since they're not part of the implementation goals, and following the pattern of other long flag handlers, I'll just return true to skip them. Adding the preserve-root flag cases... I'm thinking through how the short flag combinations work—like `-RP` parsing as R then P, or `-hR` where the recursive flag takes precedence over the dereference behavior. The logic checks out with GNU chgrp's behavior, so I'm ready to start implementing the full code.\n\nNow I'm examining the error handling for directory operations, specifically how `os.ReadDir` errors get formatted and reported to the user.\n\nThe issue is that `formatErr` always replaces the operation with \"changing group of\" regardless of what the actual operation was, so when `os.ReadDir` fails, it would incorrectly report \"changing group of 'dir'\" instead of something like \"cannot read directory\". The normalizer only matches specific error patterns like \"cannot access\" and \"changing group of\", so \"cannot read directory\" wouldn't be caught and normalized the same way. Updating the normalizer regex... I'm realizing there's an edge case with how `--` is handled in argument parsing. If someone runs `chgrp -- staff file`, the `--` sets the end-of-flags flag, which means `staff` gets treated as a filename instead of the group argument, leaving the group empty and causing a \"missing operand\" error. Let me check what GNU chgrp actually does in this scenario to make sure my implementation matches the expected behavior. The current implementation has a pre-existing bug where positional arguments after `--` get dumped into the files list instead of being parsed correctly, so `chgrp -- staff file` fails. I'll leave this as-is since it's not part of my task. For the `-R` flag interaction with `--`, the recursive option would be set before the `--` is encountered, so that should work fine.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement the actual changes. I need to add the symlink mode type and constants, update the options struct with new fields, refactor the changeGroup function to dispatch between single and recursive operations, and add several helper functions to handle the different traversal modes and permission application logic. I'll also need to update the flag parsing to recognize the new short and long options.\n\nOne thing I'm realizing is that the default symlink behavior when `-R` is used should be `-P` (don't follow symlinks), which works out naturally since that's the zero value. And the symlink flags like `-H`, `-L`, `-P` should only have an effect when `-R` is also specified, which my implementation handles automatically since those checks only happen in the recursive path.\n\nThere's a subtle edge case I need to think through though—what happens when someone passes `-L` without `-R`... without `-R`, the symlink mode is set to `symlinkL`, but since we're not in recursive mode, `chownSingle` is called, which uses `os.Chown` (follow symlinks) by default anyway. So `-L` without `-R` is effectively a no-op, which is correct.\n\nWhat about `-h` without `-R`? `-h` sets `noDereference = true`, and `chownSingle` uses `os.Lchown` when `noDereference` is true. That's correct — `-h` means \"don't follow symlinks\" regardless of recursion.\n\nAnd `-h` with `-R`? In my implementation, `chownRecursive` uses `applyChown` which is based on the symlink mode, not `noDereference`. So `-h -R` would use `lchown` (because default symlink mode is -P). But `-h -R -L` would use `chown` (because -L overrides). In GNU chgrp, `-h -R -L` is valid and `-L` takes effect, so that's correct. For just `-h -R` with no explicit -H/-L/-P flags, the default symlink mode is -P, which means `lchown`, and that's the right behavior.\n\nNow I need to think through one more edge case: what happens when the user specifies `-R` but the target is a regular file instead of a directory?\n\nWriting the code... The `statPath` function uses either `os.Stat` or `os.Lstat` depending on the follow mode, which affects how `IsDir()` behaves for symlinks — with `-P` it returns false for symlink targets, with `-L` it follows and returns true if the target is a directory, and with `-H` it follows top-level symlinks correctly. Now I'm thinking through how the `reportError` function handles errors from `stat` calls and whether they're formatted consistently with GNU chgrp.\n\nWriting the main.go structure... Continuing with the recursive logic...\n\nI'm handling the chown operation on the current path, then checking if it's a directory to recursively process its children. The applyChown function selects between following symlinks or not based on the options, and shouldFollowLink determines whether to dereference based on the symlink mode and whether we're at the top level.\n\nNow I'm implementing helper functions for the recursive traversal: statPath chooses between Stat and Lstat depending on whether we should follow symlinks, walkChildren reads directory entries and recursively applies chown to each child while capturing the first error encountered, and reportError handles error reporting.\n\nI'm noticing a potential type compatibility issue—when os.ReadDir fails, it returns an *fs.PathError, but I need to verify this matches what the error formatting expects. Actually, os.PathError and fs.PathError are the same type since Go 1.16, so the error handling should work correctly. The code compiles so it must be available. Now I'm comparing how the error formatting would look across different implementations — the Go version would produce `chgrp: ERROR_VERB '/path/to/dir': permission denied` while GNU chgrp outputs `chgrp: cannot read directory '/path/to/dir': permission denied`, and I'm noticing the verb descriptions don't quite align.\n\nI need to update the error normalizer to handle \"cannot read directory\" as a valid verb pattern, though I should first check whether this case actually appears in my planned test suite. For now I'm focusing on the core test scenarios: basic recursive chgrp, symlink handling with the -h flag, and symlink behavior with -H, -L, and -P options.\n\nNow I'm ready to start writing the code. I'll need to add the filepath import, define the symlink mode type and constants, extend the options struct with new fields, refactor the changeGroup function to dispatch based on mode, add helper functions, and update both the flag parsing logic and test cases. Let me map out the file structure to figure out where each piece should go. Adding symlink mode constants... Writing helper functions... Writing flag parsing logic...\n\nNow I'm planning out the test cases for the recursive functionality, starting with a basic test that creates a directory structure, runs the group change recursively, and verifies all files were updated.\n\nFor the symlink handling tests, I need to cover the different flag behaviors: the -P flag should only change the symlink itself without following it, -H should descend into symlinks passed as arguments, and -L should follow all symlinks throughout the tree.\n\nI also want tests for the -h flag which changes the symlink's group directly rather than its target, and I'm thinking through how to verify the default behavior where symlinks are dereferenced. The challenge with that last one is that it's hard to separately verify a symlink's own group versus its target's group, so I might need to focus on the tests where the behavior is more clearly observable.\n\nFor the actual test structure, I'll create a regular file and symlink pointing to it, set both to one group, then run chgrp with different flags and verify which one actually changed.\n\nWriting test cases... Writing test cases... I'm realizing there's a distinction between how `lchown` and `chown` behave with symlinks—`lchown` modifies the symlink itself while `chown` follows it to the target—and I need to verify that the test properly captures this by checking the symlink's group separately from the target's group using `os.Lstat`. For the recursive tests with symlinks, I need to set up a directory structure with a nested file and a symlink pointing outside, then verify how different flag combinations handle traversal and dereferencing. With `-R -P`, only the symlink itself gets its group changed without following it. With `-R -L`, the symlink is followed so the target's group changes too, and recursion would continue into it